.\" tdv(1): configure the TDV 2200 keyboard USB adapter.
.\" Written ahead of the tool: it is the specification for tdv-cli.
.Dd November 25, 2026
.Dt TDV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tdv
.Nd configure the Tandberg TDV 2200 keyboard USB adapter
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar serial
.Op Fl q | v
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm info
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm monitor
.Op Fl -raw
.Op Fl -log
.Nm
.Cm map
.Op Fl l Ar layer
.Nm
.Cm get
.Op Fl l Ar layer
.Ar key
.Nm
.Cm set
.Op Fl l Ar layer
.Ar key action
.Nm
.Cm clear
.Op Fl l Ar layer
.Ar key
.Nm
.Cm led
.Op Ar led Op Ar source
.Nm
.Cm option
.Op Ar name Op Ar value
.Nm
.Cm send
.Ar byte ...
.Nm
.Cm keys
.Nm
.Cm save | revert | defaults
.Nm
.Cm export
.Op Ar file
.Nm
.Cm import
.Op Fl -save
.Ar file
.Nm
.Cm reboot | bootloader
.Nm
.Cm completions
.Ar shell
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The TDV 2200 adapter is a Raspberry Pi Pico that connects a Tandberg
TDV 2200 terminal keyboard (Norsk Data ND 320) to a computer over USB.
To the computer it is an ordinary USB keyboard.
.Nm
talks to the adapter over a separate USB interface, so it can change the
key map and other settings while the keyboard is in use, without
restarting it or installing a driver.
.Pp
Changes take effect at once but are kept only in the adapter's memory
until
.Nm
.Cm save
writes them to its flash.
Unplugging the adapter before that brings back the saved settings.
.Ss Keys
A
.Ar key
is a position on the TDV 2200 keyboard.
It can be given as the position name from the layout document, such as
.Ql G51
or
.Ql C1 ,
or as the key's legend, such as
.Ql FUNK
or
.Ql HJELP .
Case does not matter.
Legends with more than one word can be given by their first word
.Pq Ql EKSP
or quoted in full
.Pq Ql "EKSP INNS" .
Number pad keys are written with
.Ql np ,
as in
.Ql 7np .
A legend that appears more than once, or that matches a position name,
is refused; use the position name instead.
.Nm
.Cm keys
lists every position with its legend.
.Pp
The four keys in the modifier column are named
.Cm CAPS ,
.Cm CTRL ,
.Cm LOCK
and
.Cm SHIFT .
.Ss Actions
An
.Ar action
says what a key sends.
.Bl -tag -width "layer:N"
.It Ar usage
A USB keyboard key, by name:
.Ql A ,
.Ql 1 ,
.Ql Enter ,
.Ql Escape ,
.Ql Backspace ,
.Ql Tab ,
.Ql Space ,
.Ql F1
to
.Ql F24 ,
.Ql Up ,
.Ql PageDown ,
.Ql Home ,
.Ql Insert ,
.Ql Delete ,
.Ql CapsLock ,
.Ql NonUSBackslash
and so on, or as a number from the USB HID keyboard page,
.Ql 0x68 .
.Nm
.Cm set
.Fl -help
lists the names.
Keys are named by their position on a US keyboard; the computer's
keyboard layout decides which character they type.
.It Ar modifier Ns Cm + Ns Ar usage
A key together with modifiers, for example
.Ql Ctrl+C
or
.Ql Cmd+Shift+4 .
Modifiers are
.Cm Ctrl ,
.Cm Shift ,
.Cm Alt
.Pq or Cm Option ,
.Cm Gui
.Pq or Cm Cmd , Cm Win ,
and
.Cm AltGr
.Pq right Alt ;
each can be prefixed with
.Cm Left
or
.Cm Right .
.It Ar modifier
A modifier on its own, such as
.Ql LeftAlt :
the key then works like that modifier key.
.It Ar media
A media key:
.Ql Mute ,
.Ql VolumeUp ,
.Ql VolumeDown ,
.Ql PlayPause ,
.Ql NextTrack ,
.Ql PreviousTrack ,
.Ql BrightnessUp ,
.Ql BrightnessDown .
.It Cm layer: Ns Ar N
Switches to layer
.Ar N
while the key is held.
.It Cm toggle: Ns Ar N
Switches layer
.Ar N
on or off each time the key is pressed.
.It Cm text: Ns Ar string
Types
.Ar string
when the key is pressed.
Only characters on a US keyboard can be typed this way.
.It Cm seq: Ns Ar action , Ns Ar action ...
Sends several keys one after another, for example
.Ql seq:Ctrl+A,Delete .
.It Cm none
The key sends nothing.
.El
.Pp
Quote actions that contain spaces or characters the shell treats
specially.
.Ss Layers
There are four layers, numbered 0 to 3.
Layer 0 is the normal key map.
While a higher layer is active, a key that has an action on that layer
sends it; a key with no action there sends its action from the layer
below.
Commands that take
.Fl l Ar layer
use layer 0 when it is left out.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm info
Shows the firmware version, the USB serial number, whether there are
unsaved changes, and the active layer.
.It Cm list
Lists the adapters that are plugged in, by serial number.
.It Cm monitor Op Fl -raw | -log
Shows each key event as it happens, until interrupted with Ctrl-C:
time since the previous event, the byte from the keyboard, press or
release, the key's position and legend, and what was sent to the
computer.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
+  1250 ms  2f  press    G51  FUNK            -> F13
+    96 ms  af  release  G51  FUNK            -> F13
+   310 ms  00 70        SHIFT down           -> LeftShift
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Fl -raw ,
shows only the bytes from the keyboard, as the original sniffer did.
With
.Fl -log ,
also shows the adapter's log messages.
.It Cm map Op Fl l Ar layer
Prints the key map of
.Ar layer
as a table of position, legend and action, with a mark on keys changed
from the default.
.It Cm get Oo Fl l Ar layer Oc Ar key
Prints what
.Ar key
sends.
.It Cm set Oo Fl l Ar layer Oc Ar key action
Makes
.Ar key
send
.Ar action .
.It Cm clear Oo Fl l Ar layer Oc Ar key
On layer 0, gives
.Ar key
back its default action.
On a higher layer, removes the key's action there, so it sends what it
sends on the layer below.
.It Cm led Op Ar led Op Ar source
Without arguments, shows what each LED shows.
With
.Ar led
and
.Ar source ,
makes the LED show that state.
.Ar led
is
.Cm 1
to
.Cm 8 ,
.Cm caps
or
.Cm lock .
.Ar source
is one of the computer's lock states,
.Cm caps ,
.Cm num ,
.Cm scroll ,
.Cm compose
or
.Cm kana ;
.Cm layer: Ns Ar N
for an active layer;
or
.Cm on
or
.Cm off .
By default the Caps LED shows the computer's Caps Lock state and the
others are off.
.It Cm option Op Ar name Op Ar value
Without arguments, lists the options and their values.
With
.Ar name ,
shows one; with
.Ar value
as well, changes it.
.Bl -tag -width "layer-beep"
.It Cm click Cm on | off
Key click from the keyboard's beeper on each key press.
Default
.Cm off .
.It Cm layer-beep Cm on | off
A beep when a layer is toggled on or off.
Default
.Cm off .
.El
.It Cm send Ar byte ...
Sends command bytes, in hexadecimal, straight to the keyboard, for
example
.Ql tdv send 24
to beep.
See
.Pa docs/protocol.md
for the commands.
After
.Ql 20
(reset) the adapter turns raw mode back on by itself.
Sending
.Ql 36
(raw mode off) to a keyboard with the patched original ROM makes it send
characters the adapter cannot translate, until
.Ql 31
is sent or the adapter is plugged in again.
.It Cm keys
Lists every key position with its legend and USB default.
.It Cm save
Writes the current settings to the adapter's flash, so they are used
from the next time it is plugged in.
.It Cm revert
Throws away unsaved changes and goes back to the saved settings.
.It Cm defaults
Replaces the current settings with the built-in defaults.
Use
.Cm save
afterwards to keep them.
.It Cm export Op Ar file
Writes all settings, including unsaved changes, as a TOML file, or to
standard output if
.Ar file
is left out.
.It Cm import Oo Fl -save Oc Ar file
Reads settings from a TOML file written by
.Cm export
or by hand.
Settings the file leaves out keep their defaults.
With
.Fl -save ,
also saves them.
A file with any error is refused as a whole.
.It Cm reboot
Restarts the adapter.
Unsaved changes are lost.
.It Cm bootloader
Restarts the adapter into the Pico's UF2 bootloader, where it shows up
as the
.Pa RPI-RP2
drive, ready for new firmware.
.It Cm completions Ar shell
Prints a completion script for
.Ar shell :
.Cm bash ,
.Cm zsh ,
.Cm fish
or
.Cm powershell .
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar serial , Fl -device Ar serial
Uses the adapter with this serial number, as shown by
.Cm list .
Needed only when more than one adapter is plugged in.
.It Fl l Ar layer , Fl -layer Ar layer
The layer, 0 to 3, for
.Cm map ,
.Cm get ,
.Cm set
and
.Cm clear .
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Prints only errors.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Also prints what is sent to and received from the adapter.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Prints help for
.Nm
or for a command.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Prints the version of
.Nm .
.El
.Sh FILE FORMAT
.Cm export
and
.Cm import
use TOML.
Keys are named by position, and actions are written as for
.Cm set .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# tdv export, firmware 0.2.0
version = 1

[layer.0]
G51 = "F13"            # FUNK
C99 = "LeftAlt"        # Squiggle
G1  = "text:make\\n"

[layer.1]
C8  = "Up"             # K
B4  = "Mute"           # V

[modifiers]
LOCK = "toggle:1"

[leds]
caps = "caps"
1    = "layer:1"

[options]
click = false
.Ed
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TDV_DEVICE
.It Ev TDV_DEVICE
Serial number of the adapter to use, as for
.Fl d .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
The adapter refused the command or reported an error.
.It 2
Wrong arguments, an unknown key or action, or an invalid file.
.It 3
No adapter found, or more than one and no
.Fl d .
.It 4
The adapter's firmware is too old or too new for this version of
.Nm .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Make FUNK send F13, so the computer can bind it to anything:
.Dl tdv set FUNK F13
.Pp
Use LOCK to toggle layer 1, with the arrow keys on HJKL there, and LED 1
lit while layer 1 is on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tdv set LOCK toggle:1
tdv set -l 1 H Left
tdv set -l 1 J Down
tdv set -l 1 K Up
tdv set -l 1 L Right
tdv led 1 layer:1
tdv save
.Ed
.Pp
Volume keys on P7 and P8:
.Dl tdv set P7 VolumeDown; tdv set P8 VolumeUp
.Pp
Keep a copy of the settings, then start again from the defaults:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tdv export > tdv.toml
tdv defaults
tdv save
.Ed
.Pp
Find out what a key sends, by watching while pressing it:
.Dl tdv monitor
.Sh RECOVERY
If a setting makes the keyboard unusable, hold ESC while plugging the
adapter in.
It then starts with the built-in defaults and ignores the saved
settings until
.Cm save
is used again.
.Sh NOTES
On Linux, using the adapter without root needs a udev rule, for example
in
.Pa /etc/udev/rules.d/70-tdv2200.rules :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="1209", ATTR{idProduct}=="0001", \e
  TAG+="uaccess"
.Ed
.Pp
On Windows the adapter's descriptors make Windows load its WinUSB driver
by itself.
macOS needs nothing.
